{"name":"Veronica","hex":"#a020ff","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Veronica","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Plantaginaceae – many species of herbaceous plants, many with blue flowers, including speedwells."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A female given name from Ancient Greek."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Veronica"}],"entries":[{"title":"Veronica (name)","blocks":[{"text":"Veronica is a female given name, a Latin alteration of the ancient Macedonian name Berenice (Βερενίκη), which in turn is derived from the Macedonian form of the Attic Greek Φερενίκη, Phereníkē, or Φερονίκη, Pheroníkē, from φέρειν, phérein, to bring, and νίκη, níkê, \"victory\", i.e. \"she who brings victory\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Hans_Memling_026.jpg/330px-Hans_Memling_026.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veronica_(name)","wikidata":"Q20020"},{"title":"Saint Veronica","blocks":[{"text":"Saint Veronica, also known as Berenike, was a widow from Jerusalem who lived in the 1st century AD, according to extra-biblical Christian traditions. Apocryphal texts relate how Veronica was moved with sympathy seeing Jesus carrying the cross to Calvary and gave him her veil so that he could wipe his forehead. Jesus accepted the offer, and when he returned the veil the image of his face was miraculously captured on it. The resulting relic became known as the Veil of Veronica."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Hans_Memling_026.jpg/330px-Hans_Memling_026.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Veronica","wikidata":"Q196653"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/0_Ste_V%C3%A9ronique_et_les_saintes_Femmes_-_H%C3%B4tel-Dieu_%C3%A0_Cluny_-_Mac%27s.JPG/500px-0_Ste_V%C3%A9ronique_et_les_saintes_Femmes_-_H%C3%B4tel-Dieu_%C3%A0_Cluny_-_Mac%27s.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/0_Ste_V%C3%A9ronique_et_les_saintes_Femmes_-_H%C3%B4tel-Dieu_%C3%A0_Cluny_-_Mac%27s.JPG/1280px-0_Ste_V%C3%A9ronique_et_les_saintes_Femmes_-_H%C3%B4tel-Dieu_%C3%A0_Cluny_-_Mac%27s.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/0_Ste_V%C3%A9ronique_et_les_saintes_Femmes_-_H%C3%B4tel-Dieu_%C3%A0_Cluny_-_Mac%27s.JPG/500px-0_Ste_V%C3%A9ronique_et_les_saintes_Femmes_-_H%C3%B4tel-Dieu_%C3%A0_Cluny_-_Mac%27s.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Saint Veronica and the Holy Women, Grégoire Guérard, c. 1530","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A0_Ste_V%C3%A9ronique_et_les_saintes_Femmes_-_H%C3%B4tel-Dieu_%C3%A0_Cluny_-_Mac's.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/H%C3%B6galidskyrkan%2C_Stockholm_-_Veronikas_svetteduk.jpg/500px-H%C3%B6galidskyrkan%2C_Stockholm_-_Veronikas_svetteduk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/H%C3%B6galidskyrkan%2C_Stockholm_-_Veronikas_svetteduk.jpg/1280px-H%C3%B6galidskyrkan%2C_Stockholm_-_Veronikas_svetteduk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/H%C3%B6galidskyrkan%2C_Stockholm_-_Veronikas_svetteduk.jpg/500px-H%C3%B6galidskyrkan%2C_Stockholm_-_Veronikas_svetteduk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Veil of Veronica (1923) by Olle Hjortzberg in Högalid Church (Evangelical-Lutheran) in Stockholm","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AH%C3%B6galidskyrkan%2C_Stockholm_-_Veronikas_svetted